262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.007 23.14KB 6.53s
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #Yes, both `0x30` and `0x1E` are valid, official NMOS 6502 opcodes.
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #Opcode `$30` (decimal 48)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Instruction: `BMI` (Branch on Result Minus / Negative)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Addressing Mode: Relative
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Byte Length: 2 bytes (`30 nn`)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Cycles:
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * 2 cycles (branch not taken)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * 3 cycles (branch taken, same page)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * 4 cycles (branch taken across page boundary)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Operation: Branches to `PC + offset` if the Negative flag (N) is set (`1`).
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #Opcode `$1E` (decimal 30)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Instruction: `ASL` (Arithmetic Shift Left)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Addressing Mode: Absolute, X-indexed (`$aaaa,X`)
262e019a [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Byte Length: 3 bytes (`1E LL HH`)
262e019b [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Cycles: 7 cycles (always takes 7 cycles, even without a page boundary cross, as it is a
262e019b [gemini-3.7-flash] #read-modify-write instruction)
262e019b [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Operation: Shifts the contents of memory address `(HHLL + X)` left by 1 bit. Bit 7 goes
262e019b [gemini-3.7-flash] #into the Carry flag (C), and bit 0 becomes `0`. Sets the N and Z flags based on the result.
262e019b [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e7014 [simon@midnightblue.local] opcode $5e?
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.007 24.33KB 2.18s
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #Yes, opcode `$5E` is also a valid, official NMOS 6502 opcode.
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #Opcode `$5E` (decimal 94)
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Instruction: `LSR` (Logical Shift Right)
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Addressing Mode: Absolute, X-indexed (`$aaaa,X`)
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Byte Length: 3 bytes (`5E LL HH`)
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Cycles: 7 cycles (fixed cycle count because it is a read-modify-write instruction)
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Operation: Shifts the memory byte at `(HHLL + X)` right by 1 bit.
262e703e [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Bit 0 shifts into the Carry flag (C).
262e703f [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Bit 7 is cleared to `0`.
262e703f [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Flags affected:
262e703f [gemini-3.7-flash] #    * N (Negative): Always set to `0`.
262e703f [gemini-3.7-flash] #    * Z (Zero): Set if result is `0`, cleared otherwise.
262e703f [gemini-3.7-flash] #    * C (Carry): Holds the old bit 0.
262e703f [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e7919 [simon@midnightblue.local] does pet key repeat depend on hw timer?
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.011 25.23KB 7.16s
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• No, key repeat does not use a dedicated hardware timer. It is handled entirely in
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #software* via countdown counters inside the standard 60 Hz system interrupt handler
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #(`IRQ`).
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #How Key Repeat Works in PET Kernal / Hardware
262e7993 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. The 60 Hz IRQ Tick:
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * An interrupt is triggered 60 times per second (50 Hz on PAL models), generated by the CRT
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #vertical blank signal wired to the 6520 PIA 1 (`CB1`/`CA1` line) or 6522 VIA.
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * On each interrupt tick, the Kernal calls the keyboard scan routine (`SCNKEY`).
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Software Repeat Counters:
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * When a key is held down across multiple IRQ ticks, the Kernal decrements software delay
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #countdown bytes in low RAM:
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #     * Initial Delay Counter: Waits for a hold delay (typically ~16–30 ticks / ~0.5
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #seconds).
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #     * Repeat Speed Counter: Once active, pushes the key into the keyboard buffer every 4–6
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #ticks.
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * If `IRQ` ticks stop occurring, key repeats and keyboard buffering stop functioning.
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. PET Graphics vs. Business Models:
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Graphics Keyboard (PET 2001 / 3000): Only certain keys (or all keys depending on
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Kernal version and the repeat flag at memory location `$028A` / `650`) repeat automatically in
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #software.
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Business Keyboard (4000 / 8000 series): Has a dedicated physical `RPT` key
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #wired directly into the keyboard matrix. Holding a key down together with `RPT` tells the software
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #scan routine to bypass the initial delay and repeat immediately.
262e7994 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
