262ef668 [simon@midnightblue.local] does cbm pet have audio?
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.003 503B 4.27s
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The short answer is: not natively on the earliest models, but yes on later revisions and via
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #simple hardware additions.
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Here is how audio works across the Commodore PET line:
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Original PET 2001 (1977):
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  These models did not include an internal speaker or dedicated sound chip. However, the system's
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #MOS 6522 VIA (Versatile Interface Adapter) chip could generate tones using its shift
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #register output on pin CB2. Users commonly connected an external piezo buzzer or amplifier to the
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #User Port (pins CB2 and Ground) to get 1-channel sound in games and utilities.
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Later PET / CBM Models (2001-N, 4000, 8000 series):
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  Later revisions included a built-in piezo beeper connected internally to the 6522 VIA. While it
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #was primarily intended for system beeps and alerts, software could control the frequency and output
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #simple one-voice melodies or basic sound effects.
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Sound Capabilities:
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  The audio is limited to simple square-wave tones (or rudimentary sample playback through fast
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #software bit-banging), as the PET does not have a dedicated synthesizer chip like the SID chip
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #found in the later Commodore 64.
262ef6b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
